<interrogationes unitas="tabellarius">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I write a JSON-RPC server loop over stdin and stdout?
    <responsum symbolum="tabellarius_lineam_legere tabellarius_nuntium_legere tabellarius_responsum tabellarius_lineam_scribere piscina_generare_dynamicum piscina_destruere" via="gesta/fontes/tabularium.c gesta/instrumenta/fori_principale.c">Make
      one piscina per message, read a message, classify it, answer,
      then destroy (or empty) the piscina. For MCP-style line framing:
      tabellarius_lineam_legere, then tabellarius_nuntium_legere, then
      tabellarius_responsum or tabellarius_errorem written back with
      tabellarius_lineam_scribere. For LSP framing use
      tabellarius_epistulam_legere and tabellarius_epistulam_scribere
      instead. Every result points into that piscina, so it dies with
      it. The main loop at the end of gesta/fontes/tabularium.c is a
      complete line-framed server (piscina_generare_dynamicum per
      message, piscina_destruere after); fori_principale.c shows the
      same loop as a proxy that forwards requests instead of
      answering them.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I tell end of input apart from a bad message?
    <responsum symbolum="tabellarius_epistulam_legere tabellarius_lineam_legere tabellarius_nuntium_legere">Both
      readers return an empty chorda and set *fluxus_finitus to VERUM
      both at EOF and when the stream is corrupt (a header line of 255
      bytes or more counting its \r, no or a negative Content-Length,
      short body, body over TABELLARIUS_CORPUS_MAXIMUM, or a last line
      without a newline; empty lines are skipped, not errors).
      Either way the framing is lost, so the caller should exit, not
      try to resync. Test *fluxus_finitus, not the chorda: a
      Content-Length of 0 also gives an empty body, with
      *fluxus_finitus FALSUM. A Content-Length value that is not a
      number is NOT detected: strtol reads it as 0 (or its numeric
      prefix), the body is taken as empty and the stream silently
      desyncs. A message that frames correctly but is not valid
      JSON-RPC comes back from tabellarius_nuntium_legere as
      TABELLARIUS_PRAVUM (radix NIHIL if the JSON did not parse); answer
      it with an error.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what are PETITIO, NUNTIATIO, RESPONSUM and PRAVUM?
    <responsum symbolum="tabellarius_nuntium_legere">The
      message kinds, judged only by structure (the jsonrpc field is not
      checked): a string method with an id is a PETITIO (request, must
      be answered); a string method without an id is a NUNTIATIO
      (notification, never answered); an id with result or error and no
      string method is a RESPONSUM; anything else is PRAVUM, including batches
      (JSON arrays), which this library does not support. A JSON null id
      still counts as present.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why is the request id kept as a JsonValor instead of a number?
    <responsum symbolum="tabellarius_nuntium_legere tabellarius_responsum tabellarius_errorem">JSON-RPC
      allows the id to be an integer or a string, and the reply must
      carry it back exactly. Converting it would corrupt it (a string id
      or a big integer), so TabellariusNuntius.id is the parsed JSON
      node, verbatim, and you pass it straight to tabellarius_responsum
      or tabellarius_errorem. NIHIL there writes id null.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I send pretty-printed JSON with line framing?
    <responsum symbolum="tabellarius_lineam_scribere tabellarius_epistulam_scribere">No.
      With line framing one message is one line, so
      tabellarius_lineam_scribere refuses a body that contains a raw
      newline: it prints a complaint to stderr, writes nothing and
      returns FALSUM (it also returns FALSUM for an empty body). Write
      compact JSON, which escapes newlines inside strings. LSP framing
      (tabellarius_epistulam_scribere) has a byte count, so any body is
      fine there.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can a hostile client make the reader allocate huge amounts of memory?
    <responsum symbolum="tabellarius_epistulam_legere tabellarius_lineam_legere piscina_allocare">Partly.
      With LSP framing the Content-Length is checked against
      TABELLARIUS_CORPUS_MAXIMUM (64 MB) before allocating, but a claim
      just under the cap is allocated in full from the piscina before
      any body byte arrives (and piscina_allocare exits the process if
      that piscina cannot grow). With line framing the cap is checked
      only after the whole line has been read into memory, so a line
      that never ends grows without limit.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I send a notification or an error reply?
    <responsum symbolum="tabellarius_nuntiationem tabellarius_errorem">tabellarius_nuntiationem
      builds the method plus optional params object (params NIHIL omits
      the field); tabellarius_errorem builds an error reply from the
      id, a code and a C string message. Both return the body as a
      chorda in your piscina (empty if a required argument is NIHIL);
      frame it yourself with the matching scribere function. Standard
      codes are TABELLARIUS_ERROR_ANALYSIS (-32700) through
      TABELLARIUS_ERROR_INTERNUS (-32603), plus the LSP code
      TABELLARIUS_ERROR_NONDUM_INITIATUM (-32002).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
